\]
of the Brauer tree $T$, where one of $u=v_{2}$, $v_{3}=v_{2}$ may occur (but cannot be both as $u\ne v_3$), 
and any of $b=e$, $c=e$ and $b=c=e$ may hold. 
\end{itemize}
Then, we do not specify the distinguished vertex and we define $\Gamma^{(2)}(T,v_{1},v_{2})$.

Hence, $T$ coincides with the cycle of odd length. Its Brauer quiver $Q_{T}$ is given by
\[
\begin{xy}
(0,11)*[o]+{1}="0",(15,5)*[o]+{2}="1",(20,-8)*[o]+{3}="2", (-15,5)*[oo]+{2n+1}="3",(-20,-8)*[o]+{2n}="4",
(17,-20)*[o]+{4}="5",(0,-30)*{}="dammy",
\ar @<1mm> "0";"1"^{\alpha_1}
\ar @<1mm> "1";"0"^{\alpha_2}
\ar @<1mm> "1";"2"^{\beta_2}
\ar @<1mm> "2";"1"^{\beta_3}
\ar @<1mm> "0";"3"^{\gamma_{1}}
\ar @<1mm> "3";"0"^{\gamma_{2n+1}}
\ar @<1mm> "4";"3"^{\beta_{2n}}
\ar @<1mm> "3";"4"^{\beta_{2n+1}}
\ar @<1mm> "2";"5"^{\alpha_{3}}
